## Overview

`service-notification` provides the core Effect notification dispatcher. It supports two send forms: channel-only messages such as `{ email: ... }`, and structured envelopes such as `{ persist, message }`. The structured form lets the service call a consumer-provided `NotificationPersistenceStore` so inbox notifications and delivery queue snapshots are persisted by `send` before optional channel dispatch.

The core package intentionally does not include provider-specific dependencies, database clients, migrations, authorization, or worker runtime ownership. Install separate channel registry entries, such as `notification-channel-email-ses`, to add transports. Use `notification-persistence` for reusable table definitions when you want durable inbox and delivery queue storage.

## Included Files

- `services/notification/index.ts` - Main `NotificationService`, persistence-store contract, and layer helpers
- `services/notification/channels/index.ts` - Channel contract, channel registry, and structured send input types
- `services/notification/schema.ts` - Dynamic message envelope and shared send error

## Channel Dispatch

```ts
const notifications = yield* NotificationService;

yield* notifications.send({
  email: {
    to: "user@example.com",
    subject: "Deploy succeeded",
    html: "<h1>main -> production</h1><p>All checks passed.</p>",
  },
});
```

## Persistence-Aware Send

Provide a `NotificationPersistenceStore` implementation that writes to your app-owned database transaction or repository. Then call `send` with a structured envelope.

```ts
const notificationServiceLayer = NotificationService.makePersistentLayer({
  channels: [emailChannel],
  store: {
    persist: (input) => saveNotificationAndDeliveries(input),
  },
});

yield* notifications.send({
  persist: {
    idempotencyKey: "application:123:submitted",
    eventKey: "application.submitted",
    recipientUserId: "user_123",
    inbox: {
      title: "Application submitted",
      href: "/applications/123",
    },
    deliveries: [
      {
        channel: "email",
        purpose: "transactional",
        recipientAddress: "user@example.com",
        payload: {
          to: "user@example.com",
          subject: "Application submitted",
          text: "Thanks for applying.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
});
```

The service owns orchestration. The consumer still owns trusted recipient lookup, authorization, database writes, migrations, retry workers, and any app-specific correlation fields.

## Typed Messages

`NotificationService.send` remains typed from installed channel payload declarations through `NotificationChannels`. Structured envelopes add a generic `persist` payload without forcing a database dependency into the registry item.
